atter","volume":"29"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85020832059"],"search":{"DAF":["Aarhus University","All Danish Affiliations","All Miscellaneous","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-OT","DI-OT-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Miscellaneous","Other Research","Technical University of Denmark","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["au-cdcccac0-372d-4ca6-baa4-94289ec6df84","dtu-8bafd981-d003-49e4-8c4e-fb5485d24056","ku-4b8df35b-1c92-4bc3-acdb-7b9160da3760"],"src":["AU Aarhus University","DTU Technical University of Denmark","KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"The Atomic Simulation Environment - A Python library for working with atoms","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S03-094.79-027601","year":"2017","yearsub":"2017"}
{"abstract":"Building on literary theory and data from a field study of text in chemotherapy, this article introduces the concept of intertext and the associated concepts of corpus and intertextuality to CSCW. It shows that the ensemble of documents used and produced in practice can be said to form a corpus of written texts. On the basis of the corpus, or subsections thereof, the actors in cooperative work create intertext between relevant (complementary) texts in a particular situation, for a particular purpose. The intertext of a particular situation can be constituted by several kinds of intertextuality, including the complementary type, the intratextual type and the mediated type. In this manner the article aims to systematically conceptualise cooperative actors’ engagement with text in text-laden practices. The approach is arguably novel and beneficial to CSCW. The article also contributes with a discussion of computer enabling the activity of creating intertext. This is a key concern for cooperative work as intertext is central to text-centric work practices such as healthcare.","author_count":1,"contributors":["Christensen, Lars Rune"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-382555"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"1 contributor","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Computer technology","Documents","Healthcare","Intertext","Literary theory","Text"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"on intertext in chemotherapy an ethnography of text in medical practice","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"rem","url":"https://www.researchgate.net/profile/Lars_Christensen11/publication/284930271_On_Intertext_in_Chemotherapy_an_Ethnography_of_Text_in_Medical_Practice/links/572764ec08ae262228b44ab2.pdf","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-382555","identifier":[{"id":"84948701244","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-56fe8b97-93b9-439a-b3c7-e670eecf6ebf"],"keyword":["Computer technology","Documents","Healthcare","Intertext","Literary theory","Text"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-0C01C43300C87D49430009E3E0866BB4"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"rem","url":"https://www.researchgate.net/profile/Lars_Christensen11/publication/284930271_On_Intertext_in_Chemotherapy_an_Ethnography_of_Text_in_Medical_Practice/links/572764ec08ae262228b44ab2.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/82503264/IntertextChristensen2016.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2016","yearSub":"2016"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Business IT","IT University of Copenhagen, Technologies in Practice, Technologies in Practice","The Department, Interdisciplinary Section"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Lars Rune","key":"christensenpau","last":"Christensen","locid":"92f6c3f3-0ad2-40fc-b278-69170f87567d","name":"Christensen, Lars Rune","orcid":"0000-0001-7204-465X","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1007/s10606-015-9238-1","issn":["09259724","15737551"],"issn_org":["09259724"],"issue":"1","journal":"Computer Supported Cooperative Work","pages":"1-38","publisher":"Springer","status":"p","title":"Computer Supported Cooperative Work","volume":"25"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-84948701244"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-56fe8b97-93b9-439a-b3c7-e670eecf6ebf"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"On Intertext in Chemotherapy: An Ethnography of Text in Medical Practice","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-382555","year":"2016","yearsub":"2016"}
{"abstract":"Building on data from fieldwork at a medical department, this paper focuses on the varied nature of computational artifacts in practice. It shows that medical practice relies on multiple heterogeneous computational artifacts that form complex constellations. In the hospital studied the computational artifacts are both coordinative, image-generating, and intended for the control of nuclear-physical and chemical processes. Furthermore, the paper entails a critique of the notion of ‘computer support’, for not capturing the diverse constitutive powers of computer technology; its types if you will. The paper is a step towards establishing a lexicon of computational artifacts in practice. It is a call for a wider effort to systematically conceptualise the multiple and specifiable ways in which computational artifacts may be part of work activities. This is for the benefit of design and our understanding of work practice.","author_count":2,"contributors":["Christensen, Lars Rune","Harper, Richard"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-382174"],"event":{"acronym":"COOP","title":"International Conference on the Design of Cooperative Systems"},"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"country":["United Kingdom"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Social Shaping Research LTD"],"keyword":["Computational Artifacts","Computer Support Critique","Heterogeneous Systems","Medical Practice","Work Practice Conceptualization"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"the many faces of computational artifacts","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"id":"S01-000.00-382174","ids":["itu-3d2dde3e-1e7f-4ef5-ac1f-47f1697279b1"],"keyword":["Computational Artifacts","Computer Support Critique","Heterogeneous Systems","Medical Practice","Work Practice Conceptualization"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-3-F39E50CD267C1B9E7899A54535B61A60"],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Business IT","IT University of Copenhagen, Technologies in Practice, Technologies in Practice","The Department, TiP and Games Section"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"gb","key":"social shaping research ltd","loc":["United Kingdom","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"name":"Social Shaping Research LTD","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Lars Rune","key":"christensenpau","last":"Christensen","locid":"92f6c3f3-0ad2-40fc-b278-69170f87567d","name":"Christensen, Lars Rune","orcid":"0000-0001-7204-465X","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Richard","key":"harperpau","last":"Harper","name":"Harper, Richard","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1007/978-3-319-33464-6_6","eisbn":["9783319334646"],"isbn":["9783319334639"],"pages":"93-106","publisher":"Springer","status":"p","title":"COOP 2016: Proceedings of the 12th International Conference on the Design of Cooperative Systems, 23-27 May 2016, Trento, Italy"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-3d2dde3e-1e7f-4ef5-ac1f-47f1697279b1"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"The Many Faces of Computational Artifacts","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-382174","year":"2016","yearsub":"2016"}
{"abstract":"Objective: Patients with idiopathic rapid eye movement (REM) sleep behavior disorder (iRBD) are at high risk of developing Parkinson's disease (PD). As wake/sleep-regulation is thought to involve neurons located in the brainstem and hypothalamic areas, we hypothesize that the neurodegeneration in iRBD/PD is likely to affect wake/sleep and REM/non-REM (NREM) sleep transitions. Methods: We determined the frequency of wake/sleep and REM/NREM sleep transitions and the stability of wake (W), REM and NREM sleep as measured by polysomnography (PSG) in 27 patients with PD, 23 patients with iRBD, 25 patients with periodic leg movement disorder (PLMD) and 23 controls. Measures were computed based on manual scorings and data-driven labeled sleep staging. Results: Patients with PD showed significantly lower REM stability than controls and patients with PLMD. Patients with iRBD had significantly lower REM stability compared with controls. Patients with PD and RBD showed significantly lower NREM stability and significantly more REM/NREM transitions than controls. Conclusions: We conclude that W, NREM and REM stability and transitions are progressively affected in iRBD and PD, probably reflecting the successive involvement of brain stem areas from early on in the disease. Significance: Sleep stability and transitions determined by a data-driven approach could support the evaluation of iRBD and PD patients.","author_count":10,"contributors":["Christensen, Julie Anja Engelhard","Jennum, Poul","Koch, Henriette","Frandsen, Rune","Zoetmulder, Marielle","Arvastson, Lars Johan","Christensen, Søren Rahn","Sørensen, Helge Bjarup Dissing"],"cuid":["S02-095.74-033906"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"keyword":["Aged","Female","Humans","Journal Article","Male","Middle Aged","Neurology","Neurology clinical","Parkinson Disease","Parkinson s disease","Physiology medical","Polysomnography","REM sleep behavior disorder","Rapid eye movement sleep behavior disorder","Research Support Non U S Gov t","Sensory Systems","Sleep Stages"],"level":"sci","mra":["med","sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-PR","DI-U"],"level3":{"HO":["DI-HO-ALL","DI-HO-CAP"],"PR":["DI-PR-ALL","DI-PR-LUND"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-DTU","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-HO-CAP","DI-PR-LUND","DI-U-DTU","DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"sleep stability and transitions in patients with idiopathic rem sleep behavior disorder and patients with parkinson s disease","src":["dtu","ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-DTU","DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"loc","url":"https://backend.orbit.dtu.dk/ws/files/118436008/1_s2.0_S1388245715001716_main.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"},"id":"S02-095.74-033906","identifier":[{"id":"84926163045","type":"scopus"},{"id":"25843013","type":"pmid"}],"ids":["dtu-3acea7ce-02bc-4512-994e-0d535e4c501f","ku-6170e204-db5d-4049-8973-fdd3780389c7"],"keyword":["Aged","Data-driven detection","Female","Humans","Journal Article","Male","Middle Aged","Neurology","Neurology (clinical)","Parkinson Disease","Parkinson's disease","Physiology (medical)","Polysomnography","REM Sleep Behavior Disorder","Rapid eye movement sleep behavior disorder","Research Support, Non-U.S. Gov't","Sensory Systems","Sleep Stages","Sleep transitions"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science","Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-5-6AB44D572FEA2A42C882B47FF15C3A01"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.clinph.2015.03.006","version":"Other Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"loc","url":"https://backend.orbit.dtu.dk/ws/files/118436008/1_s2.0_S1388245715001716_main.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Medicine (MED)","Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPu