af1f3-ab3a-49b7-8b1e-5e9365ee7962","name":"Christensen, Lars Rune","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1007/s10606-012-9181-3","issn":["09259724","15737551"],"issn_org":["09259724"],"issue":"1","journal":"Computer Supported Cooperative Work","pages":"1-19","publisher":"Springer","status":"p","title":"Computer Supported Cooperative Work","volume":"23"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-84894903205"],"search":{"DAF":["Aalborg University","All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["aau-5c8d97eb-1e60-4df4-941c-608ca96d66f6"],"src":["AAU Aalborg University"],"title":"Practices of stigmergy in the building process","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-076926","year":"2014","yearsub":"2014"}
{"abstract":"Background: The golden standard for sleep classification uses manual scoring of polysomnography despite points of criticism such as oversimplification, low inter-rater reliability and the standard being designed on young and healthy subjects. New method: To meet the criticism and reveal the latent sleep states, this study developed a general and automatic sleep classifier using a data-driven approach. Spectral EEG and EOG measures and eye correlation in 1 s windows were calculated and each sleep epoch was expressed as a mixture of probabilities of latent sleep states by using the topic model Latent Dirichlet Allocation. Model application was tested on control subjects and patients with periodic leg movements (PLM) representing a non-neurodegenerative group, and patients with idiopathic REM sleep behavior disorder (iRBD) and Parkinson's Disease (PD) representing a neurodegenerative group. The model was optimized using 50 subjects and validated on 76 subjects. Results: The optimized sleep model used six topics, and the topic probabilities changed smoothly during transitions. According to the manual scorings, the model scored an overall subject-specific accuracy of 68.3 +/- 7.44 (% mu +/-sigma) and group specific accuracies of 69.0 +/- 4.62 (control), 70.1 +/- 5.10 (PLM), 67.2 +/- 8.30 (iRBD) and 67.7 +/- 9.07 (PD). Comparison with existing method: Statistics of the latent sleep state content showed accordances to the sleep stages defined in the golden standard. However, this study indicates that sleep contains six diverse latent sleep states and that state transitions are continuous processes. Conclusions: The model is generally applicable and may contribute to the research in neurodegenerative diseases and sleep disorders. (C) 2014 Elsevier B.V. All rights reserved.","author_count":12,"contributors":["Koch, Henriette","Christensen, Julie Anja Engelhard","Frandsen, Rune","Zoetmulder, Marielle","Arvastson, Lars Johan","Christensen, Søren Ro","Jennum, Poul","Sørensen, Helge Bjarup Dissing"],"cuid":["S02-096.40-033226"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"keyword":["04500 Mathematical biology and statistical methods","10515 Biophysics Biocybernetics","20004 Sense organs Physiology and biochemistry","20506 Nervous system Pathology","24500 Gerontology","Aged","Automatic sleep classification","Biochemical","Brain","Computational Biology","Electroencephalography","Electroencephalography EEG","Electrooculography","Electrooculography EOG","Eye","Female","Human Medicine Medical Sciences","Humans","Male","Middle Aged","Models and Simulations","Neurodegenerative Diseases","Neurology","Neurosciences","Nocturnal Myoclonus Syndrome","Ocular Physiological Phenomena","Parkinson Disease","Pattern Recognition Automated","Polysomnography","Primates Mammalia Vertebrata Chordata Animalia Animals Chordates Humans Mammals Primates Vertebrates Hominidae 86215 human common middle age aged female male","Probability","REM sleep behavior disorder","Sensitivity and Specificity","Signal Processing Computer Assisted","Sleep","Topic modeling"],"level":"sci","mra":["med","sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-PR","DI-U"],"level3":{"PR":["DI-PR-ALL","DI-PR-LUND"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-DTU","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-PR-LUND","DI-U-DTU","DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"automatic sleep classification using a data driven topic model reveals latent sleep states","src":["dtu","ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-DTU","DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jneumeth.2014.07.002","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S02-096.40-033226","identifier":[{"id":"84904872865","type":"scopus"},{"id":"25016288","type":"pmid"}],"ids":["dtu-1d8c2f64-8517-4868-9a2f-6fab0c390dd5","ku-d05adacd-152d-4c90-bdfd-8d0feb4f6ee8"],"keyword":["04500, Mathematical biology and statistical methods","10515, Biophysics - Biocybernetics","20004, Sense organs - Physiology and biochemistry","20506, Nervous system - Pathology","24500, Gerontology","Aged","Automatic sleep classification","BIOCHEMICAL","Brain","Computational Biology","EEG electroencephalography clinical techniques, diagnostic techniques","Electroencephalography","Electroencephalography (EEG)","Electrooculography","Electrooculography (EOG)","Eye","Female","Human Medicine, Medical Sciences","Humans","Male","Middle Aged","Models and Simulations","NEUROSCIENCES","Neurodegenerative diseases","Neurology","Nocturnal Myoclonus Syndrome","Ocular Physiological Phenomena","Parkinson Disease","Parkinson's disease nervous system disease","Pattern Recognition, Automated","Polysomnography","Primates Mammalia Vertebrata Chordata Animalia (Animals, Chordates, Humans, Mammals, Primates, Vertebrates) - Hominidae [86215] human common middle age, aged female, male","Probability","REM Sleep Behavior Disorder","Sensitivity and Specificity","Signal Processing, Computer-Assisted","Sleep","Sleep state switching","Topic modeling","electrooculography clinical techniques, diagnostic techniques","eye sensory system","idiopathic REM sleep behavior disorder nervous system disease","latent sleep state","optimized sleep model mathematical and computer techniques","periodic leg movement nervous system disease","topic model Latent Dirichlet Allocation mathematical and computer techniques"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science","Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-5-E52125CC8ED79F8126CA7E40D0AC4503"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jneumeth.2014.07.002","version":"Other Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Unused","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2014","yearSub":"2014"},"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"dk","key":"dtu technical university of denmark","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-DTU","l3nl":"Technical University of Denmark","l3nn":"DTU Technical University of Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"DTU Technical University of Denmark","namevar":["Technical University of Denmark"],"nora_code":"DI-U-DTU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"dk","key":"lundbeck","l2":"DI-PR","l2n":"Private Research","l3":"DI-PR-LUND","l3nl":"Lundbeck Pharma","l3nn":"Lundbeck","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Lundbeck","namevar":["H. Lundbeck A/S"],"nora_code":"DI-PR-LUND","used":1},{"affno":"3","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Health and Medical Sciences, Center for Healthy Ageing","Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Health and Medical Sciences, Department of Clinical Medicine, Department of Clinical Medicine"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Henriette","key":"kochhenriettepau","last":"Koch","name":"Koch, Henriette","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Julie Anja Engelhard","key":"christensenjulieanjaengelhardpau","last":"Christensen","locid":"5201365e-8d40-4d6b-af99-026b1108b5e9","name":"Christensen, Julie Anja Engelhard","orcid":"0000-0003-4225-3359","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Julie A E","key":"christensenjulieaepau","last":"Christensen","name":"Christensen, Julie A E","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Rune","key":"frandsenrunepau","last":"Frandsen","name":"Frandsen, Rune","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Marielle","key":"zoetmuldermariellepau","last":"Zoetmulder","name":"Zoetmulder, Marielle","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Lars Johan","key":"arvastsonlarsjohanpau","last":"Arvastson","locid":"d5cbd65e-7e62-4684-9fd4-8401893e739a","name":"Arvastson, Lars Johan","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Lars","key":"arvastsonlarspau","last":"Arvastson","name":"Arvastson, Lars","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Søren Ro","key":"christensensørenropau","last":"Christensen","name":"Christensen, Søren Ro","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Soren R","key":"christensensorenrpau","last":"Christensen","name":"Christensen, Soren R","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","first":"Poul","key":"jennumpoulpau","last":"Jennum","locid":"09499315-7437-47b1-a859-6783b0dd67c1","name":"Jennum, Poul","orcid":"0000-0001-6986-5254","rank":7,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Helge Bjarup Dissing","key":"sørensenhelgebjarupdissingpau","last":"Sørensen","locid":"be0efdb7-17ce-4703-b513-90dfa28f45af","name":"Sørensen, Helge Bjarup Dissing","orcid":"0000-0001-5716-7600","rank":8,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Helge B D","key":"sorensenhelgebdpau","last":"Sorensen","name":"Sorensen, Helge B D","rank":8,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["25016288"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1016/j.jneumeth.2014.07.002","issn":["01650270","1872678X"],"issn_org":["01650270"],"journal":"Journal of Neuroscience Methods","pages":"130-137","publisher":"Elsevier","status":"p","title":"Journal of Neuroscience Methods","volume":"235"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-84904872865"],"sdg":["3. Good Health and Well-being","All UN SDG Classification"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Private Research","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-PR","DI-PR-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Lundbeck Pharma","Private Research","Technical University of Denmark","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["dtu-1d8c2f64-8517-4868-9a2f-6fab0c390dd5","ku-d05adacd-152d-4c90-bdfd-8d0feb4f6ee8"],"src":["DTU Technical University of Denmark","KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Automatic sleep classification using a data-driven topic model reveals latent sleep states","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S02-096.40-033226","year":"2014","yearsub":"2014"}
{"abstract":"Background: Manual scoring of sleep relies on identifying certain characteristics in polysomnograph (PSG) signals. However, these characteristics are disrupted in patients with neurodegenerative diseases. New method: This study evaluates sleep using a topic modeling and unsupervised learning approach to identify sleep topics directly from electroencephalography (EEG) and electrooculography (EOG). PSG data from control subjects were used to develop an EOG and an EEG topic model. The models were applied to PSG data from 23 control subjects, 25 patients with periodic leg movements (PLMs), 31 patients with idiopathic REM sleep behavior disorder (iRBD) and 36 patients with Parkinson's disease (PD). The data were divided into training and validation datasets and features reflecting EEG and EOG characteristics based on topics were computed. The most discriminative feature subset for separating iRBD/PD and PLM/controls was estimated using a Lasso-regularized regression model. Results: The features with highest discriminability were the number and stability of EEG topics linked to REM and N3, respectively. Validation of the model indicated a sensitivity of 91.4% and a specificity of 68.8% when classifying iRBD/PD patients. Comparison with existing method: The topics showed visual accordance with the manually scored sleep stages, and the features revealed sleep characteristics containing information indicative of neurodegeneration. Conclusions: This study suggests that the amount of N3 and the ability to maintain NREM and REM sleep have potential as early PD biomarkers. Data-driven analysis of sleep may contribute to the evaluation of neurodegenerative patients. (C) 2014 Elsevier B.V. All rights reserved.","author_count":12,"contributors":["Christensen, Julie Anja Engelhard","Zoetmulder, Marielle","Koch, Henriette","Frandsen, Rune","Arvastson, Lars Johan","Christensen, Søren Ro","Jennum, Poul","Sørensen, Helge Bjarup Dissing"],"cuid":["S02-096.53-034446"],"filter